<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd"
                   logicalFilePath="config/liquibase/changelog/20260916211144_changelog.xml">

    <!--
    Drops ten secondary indexes that no query can use. An analysis of the database structure found
    each of them redundant for one of three reasons, none of which depends on how much data an
    instance holds.

    Group 1 - a leftmost prefix of a composite index that already exists

        submission.FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq (participation_id)
          -> idx_submission_participation_submission_date (participation_id, submission_date DESC)
        result.fk_result_submission_id (submission_id)
          -> idx_result_submission_completion_date (submission_id, completion_date DESC)
        participation.fk_participation_student_id (student_id)
          -> UK6skuc03gvp2gw5lyji5lu0v0v (student_id, exercise_id, initialization_state, attempt)
        participation.fk_participation_team_id (team_id)
          -> UC_PARTICIPATION_TEAM_ID_EXERCISE_ID_INITIALIZATION_STATE_COLS (team_id, exercise_id, initialization_state)

      With the narrow index ignored, EXPLAIN shows every lookup falling through to the composite as
      the same covering index lookup, at practically the same cost.

      All four back a foreign key and carry its name. MySQL accepts any index with the referencing
      column leftmost, so the drops succeed and the constraint survives; a foreign key whose only
      index is the one being dropped fails with ERROR 1553 instead. PostgreSQL never requires one.

      They are restricted to MySQL and PostgreSQL because H2, which the bean-instantiation startup
      check boots on, refuses the drop even when a replacement exists. Changelog 20260410144433
      restricts a drop for the same reason.

    Group 2 - too few distinct values to be selective

        result.result_rated (rated)                                 2 distinct values
        submission.jhi_type (jhi_type)                              6 distinct values
        submission.submission_discriminator (discriminator)         4 distinct values
        build_job.idx_build_job_result (build_job_result)           4 distinct values
        participation.participation_discriminator (discriminator)   3 distinct values

      Every query filtering on these also filters on exercise_id, participation_id or submission_id,
      and EXPLAIN shows the optimizer choosing that index and applying these columns as a post-filter.

      Note that idx_build_job_result indexes the build_job_result status column. The index backing
      fk_build_job_result is idx_build_job_result_id, which is kept.

    Group 3 - selective, but no query has the shape that would use it

        result.result_assessment_type (assessment_type)

      The optimizer does pick this index for a query that filters on assessment_type alone and
      selects only indexed columns. Artemis has no such query: every JPQL query mentioning
      assessmentType pairs it with a selective driver or with a date range that has its own index,
      and projects columns the index does not carry.

    All ten are created by 00000000000000_initial_schema.xml, which cannot be edited without
    invalidating recorded checksums, so - as with changelog 20260816213553 - fresh installations
    create them and drop them again here. Each changeset is one DDL guarded by an indexExists
    precondition, so a schema that never had an index marks it as run instead of failing startup,
    and each carries an explicit rollback because Liquibase cannot infer one for dropIndex.

    DROP INDEX is an INPLACE metadata-only operation on MySQL 8.0+ and PostgreSQL, so this runs in
    milliseconds regardless of table size.
    -->

    <changeSet id="20260916211144-01-drop-submission-participation-fk-index" author="krusche" dbms="mysql,postgresql">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <indexExists tableName="submission" indexName="FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq"/>
        </preConditions>
        <dropIndex tableName="submission" indexName="FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq"/>
        <rollback>
            <createIndex tableName="submission" indexName="FKeap67y7pxd6ffvwbs6d6sfhmq">
                <column name="participation_id"/>
            </createIndex>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

